Textile Order Tracking and Production Management System 
DSA Project Report 
1. Introduction 
This project focuses on designing and implementing a Textile Order Tracking and Production Management System. It is intended for textile and garment manufacturing units that need to efficiently manage customer orders, track production stages, and maintain a fast and searchable order database. 
The system supports creation and storage of textile orders, tracking of each order’s progress through multiple production stages, and searching by different keys. It also includes a custom-built file-based database with indexing implemented using B-Tree and Hashing. The backend is hosted on a cloud platform, while the frontend runs on a local client device, fulfilling the two-tier architectural requirement. 
2. Features of the Application 
2.1 Add and Store Textile Orders 
The system allows adding new orders that include the following data: 
● Order ID 
● Customer Name 
● Product Type 
● Quantity 
● Production Stage 
● Date of Order 
The order is written into a file-based database and indexed for fast retrieval. 2.2 Track Production Pipeline 
Each order passes through the following stages in the production process: 
● Cutting 
● Stitching 
● Finishing
● Packed 
A Queue is used to simulate this workflow. Orders follow a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) sequence, which reflects real factory processing patterns. 
2.3 Searching Capabilities 
Two types of search operations are supported: 
Search by Order ID (using B-Tree) 
B-Trees provide efficient and balanced search operations for numeric keys, making them suitable for database indexing. 
Search by Customer Name (using Hashing) 
A hash table is used to index customer names for constant-time average-case search performance. 
2.4 Custom File-Based Database 
All orders are stored in files rather than a traditional SQL or NoSQL database. The system maintains: 
● A raw data file containing stored order records 
● An index file with B-Tree nodes 
● A hash-index file with hash table buckets 
This satisfies the requirement of implementing a self-made database. 
2.5 Cloud-Hosted Backend with Local Frontend 
The backend is hosted on a cloud service such as Railway, Render, or AWS, and handles all business logic, data persistence, and data structure operations. 
The frontend can be implemented using any technology such as React, HTML/CSS/JavaScript, or Flutter, and it communicates with the backend through REST APIs. 
3. Use of Data Structures 
3.1 B-Tree for Order ID Indexing 
A B-Tree is used to index Order IDs. It ensures: 
● Balanced height for consistent performance 
● Logarithmic time search 
● Efficient insertion and deletion
● Suitability for disk-based indexing 
Whenever an order is added, its Order ID and file offset are inserted into the B-Tree. During search, the B-Tree provides the file offset from which the record is directly accessed. 
3.2 Hash Table for Customer Name Indexing 
Customer names are indexed using a hash table. A hash function converts the name into a bucket index. 
This allows: 
● Fast, near constant time searching 
● Handling of collisions using chaining 
● Efficient support for duplicate or similar names 
This helps users quickly locate orders by customer identity. 
3.3 Queue for Production Pipeline 
The processing of orders through different production stages is simulated using a Queue. When an order enters the factory: 
● It is enqueued into the production queue 
● When processed, it is dequeued, stage updated, and re-enqueued 
This realistically represents assembly-line order processing. 
3.4 Additional Structures 
Vectors or linked lists may be used internally to temporarily store data in memory during server operations. 
4. Backend Architecture 
The system follows a two-tier architecture. 
4.1 Backend (Cloud Hosted) 
The backend contains: 
● File-based database manager 
● B-Tree module for Order ID indexing 
● Hash table module for name indexing 
● Queue module to simulate production stages 
● REST API endpoints
Example API endpoints: 
● /addOrder 
● /searchByID 
● /searchByCustomer 
● /advanceStage 
● /getAllOrders 
The backend is hosted on a cloud platform so it can be accessed from any frontend client. 4.2 Frontend (Client-Side) 
The frontend displays order information and allows user interaction. It communicates with the backend using HTTP requests. 
It does not include heavy logic; it only sends user requests and renders backend responses. 5. File-Based Database Structure 
The database consists of three main files: 
5.1 orders.db 
Contains raw order records in serialized format. 
5.2 btree_index.db 
Contains B-Tree nodes storing: 
● Keys (Order ID) 
● Offsets (location of record in orders.db) 
5.3 name_hash_index.db 
Stores hash buckets, where each bucket contains: 
● Customer name 
● Corresponding record file offsets 
This design ensures fast reads while avoiding external database systems. 6. Technical Implementation 
6.1 Programming Languages
Backend can be implemented using C++, Python, or Node.js. 
Frontend may use HTML/CSS/JS, React, or Flutter. 
6.2 Major Modules 
1. File Manager 
Handles reading and writing records to disk, managing file offsets, and ensuring durability. 
2. B-Tree Module 
Implements insert, search, and node-splitting operations. Handles serialization of nodes to the index file. 
3. Hash Table Module 
Responsible for hashing names, managing buckets, handling collisions, and storing offsets. 
4. Queue Module 
Simulates production line state management. 
5. REST API Layer 
Connects backend logic to frontend through structured endpoints. API returns JSON responses. 
7. Conclusion 
The Textile Order Tracking and Production Management System is a practical, real-world solution that demonstrates the application of Data Structures and Algorithms in database indexing, production management, and efficient data storage. With its cloud-hosted backend, custom file-based storage system, and effective use of B-Tree, Hashing, and Queue structures, the project meets all academic requirements while remaining simple, logical, and industry-relevant.
